WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.040
- Okay, I'm Steve Anderson.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.170
I was previously the Chair
of the Linguistics Department

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.853
at Yale, which will
turn out to be relevant.

00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:23.800
It's my pleasure to introduce Gerald Shea.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.690
Gerry is not either a
linguist or a psychologist

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.570
and so perhaps it's useful for me

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.123
to give a little background.

00:00:36.287 --> 00:00:38.070
In fact, almost exactly eight years ago

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.290
I received in the mail a manuscript

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:46.210
from someone I didn't know about a topic

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.970
about which I didn't know very much.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:53.140
I'm sure Bob Frank as
current chair will confirm

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.980
the fact that we get lots of...

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.980
The Chair of Linguistics
at Yale gets lots of things

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.503
and some of them are a little unusual.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.940
But this one seemed to be
worth a lot more attention

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.940
and not only because the
author was a Yalie, BA 64.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:17.623
The manuscript I had, which
was called Language of Light.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.253
No, it was called a Song Without Words.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:26.070
It was interesting for two
somewhat different reasons.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.430
On the one hand it
described the experience

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.430
of someone whose language
experience is rather different

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.420
from any of the ones
that cognitive scientists

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.170
tend to study.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.290
On the one hand we study hearing people

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.465
who learn spoken languages
and on the other hand

00:01:43.465 --> 00:01:47.970
we study Deaf people
who learn sign languages

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.434
but here was the experience of someone

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:54.660
who had lost a great deal of his hearing

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.470
at a very early age, but that
fact hadn't actually been

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.140
properly diagnosed
until much later in life

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.490
and he succeeded in the hearing community

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.650
despite his hearing deficit.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:09.550
Getting through Yale

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.693
and getting through Columbia Law School.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.910
This seemed to me was a
situation that cognitive science

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.300
ought to pay attention to.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.680
It's rather unusual to characterize

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.860
the linguistic experience
of someone functioning

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.240
in a spoken language who has
actually very limited access

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.550
to speech as a consequence
of the hearing deficit.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.830
So on the one hand that struck
me as an interesting problem

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.720
for cognitive scientists.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.950
On the other hand the book
contained a great deal

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.820
of really interesting information

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:45.820
about the history of the
emergence and development

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.550
of signed language in
France, which as we know

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.600
is the background out of which ASL comes,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.380
work that was based on
Gerry's work in the libraries

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.610
and archives in France in
which taught me a great deal.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.720
I mean I had done some
reading on this background

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.010
but I learned a lot from
looking at this manuscript.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.640
So I was happy to encourage it

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.240
and to recommend it for publication

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.930
and to provide some comments,

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.520
especially on the linguistic aspects.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.260
As I've said, Gerry isn't a linguist

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.750
and there were some things that I thought

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.750
needed a little bit of commentary.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.420
But then in 2013, the book was published

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.203
as Song Without Words.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.730
The book is lovely and an eloquent book.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.230
If you haven't read it, you surely should.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.510
But when I saw what came out in print

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:43.510
I was somewhat less happy at
the fact that all this stuff

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.760
about the science of sign and
the history had been omitted

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:54.500
and this was almost entirely
about Gerry's experience

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.453
as a partially hearing person.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.270
Well, I talked to him
about this and he said

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.180
that's because the
publishers wanted this to be

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.680
a general audience book
and they said take all that

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.663
science and history stuff out of it.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:12.325
People will like it more if
it's just a lovely experience.

00:04:12.325 --> 00:04:14.770
It is a wonderful book,
but I'm afraid that because

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.730
that material was omitted,
it's attracted less attention

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.900
from linguists and cognitive scientists

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.400
than perhaps it ought to have.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.600
But anyway, I was very
happy a few years later

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.847
when I heard from Gerry
that he was interested

00:04:27.847 --> 00:04:31.830
in taking the material from
his original manuscript

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.510
that hadn't been included, expanding it,

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.690
and publishing that as a separate book.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.380
So I was happy to put
him in touch with people

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.630
at Yale University Press.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.160
The result is an absolutely wonderful book

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.400
on the historical origins and development

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.260
of signed languages and the
Deaf community in France

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.160
and subsequently in this country.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.770
If you haven't read Language of Light

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.100
then you should certainly
read that one too.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.110
So without further ado,
I will turn this over

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.120
to Gerry who will, I gather,
be giving you material

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.460
mostly from the second book.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.949
Thank you.

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:15.949
(audience applauds)

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.460
- Thank you very much, Steve,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.980
and thank you all for coming.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.753
I think I'll take this device off.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.020
Thanks to Raffaella
Zanuttini and her staff

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.600
for organizing this conference so ably

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.050
and it's really a delight to be here

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:40.720
as it was to hear the two prior speakers.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.863
I learned a lot in the first
hour and a half of today.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:51.030
As Steve explained, I
first became familiar

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.320
with the history and languages of the Deaf

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.360
when I was looking for information
about the partially Deaf

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.373
for my first book, the
memoir that Steve mentioned.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.600
As I discuss at length in that
book, I am partially Deaf,

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.840
a condition that arose following

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.110
an extended childhood illness.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.020
When others speak, I
hear an elusive language

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.880
to which in a rapid course of conversation

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.473
I endeavored to give meaning.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.060
The words of this language,
what I call its lyricals,

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:29.120
a term of my invention
are transitional words,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.750
wrong words, non-words.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.470
That in lieu of those actually spoken

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:37.733
register in the minds
of the partially Deaf.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.200
The high frequency and the soft consonants

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.900
are usually missing.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.510
The vowels and the occasional
consonant aren't enough.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:50.460
In my life and the lives of
the partially Deaf like me

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.250
are a constant unscrambling of language

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:56.333
punctuated by masks of understanding.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:01.050
I communicate fairly
effectively using the hearing

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.878
I've retained, lip reading, hearing aids,

00:07:03.878 --> 00:07:08.663
and an assortment of
assistive listening devices.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:14.580
There's no time here
to dwell on my history.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.850
It's all in Song Without
Words if you want to buy it

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:20.603
on Amazon and it's all in there.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.603
When I finally did get
hearing aids at age 34,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.710
a lot of sounds came back to me that were

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.550
the sounds of childhood.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.960
Crickets, birds, not seagulls
which I've always heard

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.943
but, you know, birds twerping in the air.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.240
Piccolos, high stringed
instruments that brought

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.500
the melody back to
music for me rather than

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.733
the counter point of low instruments.

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:51.674
As to water, water is a beautiful sound.

00:07:51.674 --> 00:07:55.890
When I was presenting the
book at a gathering in London,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.020
the first book, a Song Without Words,

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.500
a reporter Hayley Campbell
from the New Statesman

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.810
came up to me and said
hey, tell me something

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:03.870
that I haven't...

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.640
You know, that's not the
critics don't talk about it,

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.404
that nobody knows about it.

00:08:08.404 --> 00:08:11.520
And I said, well, I'll tell
you a little bit about water.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.350
When I got hearing aids,
the first day I got them

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:18.670
I went off to pee and I had them on

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:23.670
and it was the first time
I realized since childhood

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.740
but I had forgotten
that, that when you pee

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.343
it actually makes a sound.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.200
So Hayley Campbell in the New
Statesman, she liked that.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:35.720
And the New Statesman,
these aren't my words,

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.880
of course they're hers, she
said imagine what it would be

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.070
like going through life without knowing

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:43.746
what piss sounded like.

00:08:43.746 --> 00:08:46.746
(audience laughing)

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:49.070
This room scares me to death.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:49.970
One more anecdote.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.310
I think I was in this room
the very first lecture

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:56.410
I was at Yale and the
teacher started to speak,

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.270
I don't know, it was probably history.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.233
Something easy.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.580
When he started to speak,
everybody picked up

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.700
their pen and pencil, and
of course, I could hear

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:09.670
his voice and a few words,
but they were actually

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:11.920
starting to write and
I thought, this must be

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.380
a very good university since the students

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:15.990
are still writing.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.860
So my theme song might've
been something like

00:09:20.407 --> 00:09:24.523
Gaudeamus Igitur since we
can't even hear the teachers.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.490
Moving on to the history now.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.930
I have no fluent understanding
of the signed languages

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.630
of the profoundly Deaf, but the grace

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:37.310
and visual clarity of
those who communicate

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.240
in sign languages as you have just seen

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:43.993
and as you are seeing
now, are to me, a wonder.

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:48.680
I feel a close affinity to them.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:53.680
Theirs is not an unplanned,
but a natural visual poetry

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:57.350
at once both the speech
and the music of the Deaf.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:58.880
Though I live in the realm of the hearing,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.750
a part of my life in the form of my search

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:05.510
for communicative grace
and clarity is quartered

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:08.210
in my understanding of
the world of the Deaf

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:09.993
and I feel as if a part of it.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.870
As I've mentioned, I
originally became familiar

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.620
with the history and languages of the Deaf

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:19.550
when I was looking for
information about people like me.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.870
I found that history
so rich and intriguing

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:25.173
and fraught with pathos that
I decided to write about it.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:29.640
The Language of Light, my
book covers a long history

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.410
from the classical period
through our own day

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:34.600
in about 200 pages.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:38.770
I can't talk about all
of that in half an hour,

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:41.430
thus I'd like to focus on what I consider

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.790
the centerpiece of the history of the Deaf

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:48.030
and signed languages,
I.E. the accomplishments

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:51.630
of the great French teachers
of the early 19th century,

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.150
notably Auguste Bébian.

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:56.660
This is also the heart of the book.

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:01.090
I call this period the
Enlightenment, or the Age of Bébian.

00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:04.590
But I'll also mention in
unforgivable nutshell form

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:08.280
the time before and the
time after that era.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.630
The Age of Bébian as we
shall see was an island

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:16.200
of eloquence and wisdom in a
wide, rapidly running river

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:19.063
of ignorance and prejudice
on the part of the hearing.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:23.580
A brief note first on what
history and linguistics

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.970
have taught us about signed languages

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:29.750
and we've heard much of that this morning.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:32.010
Children born profoundly Deaf, of course,

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.270
hear virtually nothing at all.

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:36.920
They're unable to speak
because their ears can't

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:39.880
transmit to the brain
the information it needs

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:43.700
to shape the imitative
speaking patterns of the voice.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.930
These children then turn
instinctively to gestures

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.750
in order to communicate with others.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.510
They do so extraordinarily
and efficiently.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:54.850
Once they are exposed to a signed language

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.133
which they learn quickly and naturally.

00:12:00.777 --> 00:12:02.560
They're very soon able exactly like

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.770
their hearing counterparts
to express a limitless number

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:09.741
of ideas in well-formed sentences

00:12:09.741 --> 00:12:12.403
with impeccable syntax.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:16.320
The discourse of the Deaf
is not as in the case

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.360
of spoken languages
configured by the tongue

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:22.048
and other organs of
speech, arranging molecules

00:12:22.048 --> 00:12:26.200
in the air for the ear,
but by the motion of hands

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:29.460
and other gestures that
are transmitted by light

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.940
to the eyes of their interlocutors,

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:34.473
hence my title the Language of Light.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.350
Signed languages are just as sophisticated

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.180
as our spoken tongue.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.880
They have their own linguistics unit

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:44.803
just as our oral languages do.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:49.220
When Deaf individuals
render or interpret signs

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:51.520
they use the same regions of the brain

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.480
that the hearing do when they speak

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:54.760
and listen to speech.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:57.593
They are hearing their language.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.340
There is little historical
record of the Deaf

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:05.193
or their languages
before the 16th century,

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:09.060
but signed languages in
various forms and degrees

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.130
of sophistication have probably existed

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:15.440
for at the very least, 100,000 years.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:19.530
For as long as mankind
and thus have Deaf people.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.210
Signed languages were
not invented by anyone,

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:24.923
nor was French, English, or German.

00:13:26.276 --> 00:13:29.373
So the time before Bébian.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.650
Aristotle found the Blind
more intelligent than the Deaf

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:36.930
because the Blind could hear.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:39.240
He believed that all rational discourse

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:40.503
had to be audible.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:43.080
Sandy Gusten thought Deaf people

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.210
could never learn to read.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:49.307
The Justinian Code in 522
provide that if a person

00:13:49.307 --> 00:13:51.510
was Deaf from birth and couldn't speak

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.480
he had virtually no rights.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.757
Diderot thought that to teach the Deaf

00:13:56.757 --> 00:14:01.050
abstractions was an insurmountable task.

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:04.800
Juan Pablo Bonet in 17th century Spain

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.890
called the congenitally
Deaf monsters of nature

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:11.840
and human only in form.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.370
His goal was to cure them by teaching them

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.210
how to speak.

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:19.610
He failed, of course, as
did all of his Spanish,

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:22.740
British, Dutch, French, German,

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.780
and American Oralist successors.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.990
It was virtually impossible for
a person to speak distinctly

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:32.880
if since birth or early
infancy he or she had not

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:33.780
been able to hear.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:37.810
Lip reading is just as difficult.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:39.700
While I and other partially Deaf people

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.670
lip read bit, you have to be able to hear

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:45.980
in order to do it well,

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:49.423
or as well as we do
which is not very well.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:53.990
So forget about all those Deaf characters

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:56.880
reading lips for the CIA
in those movies you see

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:59.920
through hermetically sealed
windows in a telescope.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.620
They're just reciting a
script that they've memorized.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.340
I did though manage to do it once.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.360
I was watching Bill Clinton play golf

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:11.223
with Greg Norman I think,
who wore that, you know,

00:15:11.223 --> 00:15:13.250
straw hat and it was the party afterwards

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:16.840
somewhere in the states and
Clinton stepped backwards

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:20.350
and fell over the porch and broke his leg.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.110
They wheeled him out to Air Force One

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.430
to set the leg in Washington.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:27.250
The leg was sticking out and
it was two kilometers out

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:30.370
on the tarmac but the
cameras with their big lenses

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:33.360
were there watching him, but no sound.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:34.773
Clinton said.

00:15:38.419 --> 00:15:39.252
(audience laughing)

00:15:39.252 --> 00:15:42.090
Now, the first three
words are clear, right?

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.100
What the F are the first three words.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:45.960
But what's the rest?

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:46.793
What is.

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:50.550
Now, no one in this room has an idea.

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:54.730
Sometimes with context you can get it

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:57.250
and what he said was what the F

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:59.253
are those photographers doing there?

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:01.860
I was very proud of myself

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:04.424
but it was a once in a lifetime event.

00:16:04.424 --> 00:16:06.280
(audience laughing)

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.450
So what did Bonet think
about Sign Language?

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:10.560
He said if you placed two Deaf mutes

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.170
in each other's presence,
meeting for the first time,

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.797
they can communicate because
they use the same signs.

00:16:17.797 --> 00:16:19.180
(speaking in foreign language)

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:21.690
But he, like others, was
not interested in trying

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:24.820
to understand these
signs, or to ask himself

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:27.190
what the Deaf were saying to each other,

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.500
or where their signs came from,

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:31.800
or whether these
communications might be a form

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.410
of language, indeed, because it interfered

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.760
with their learning the
speech they weren't learning.

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:41.340
He found it important as
alas, many speech therapists

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.850
and doctors do today,

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:48.163
quote never to let the
mute use it unquote.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:52.900
Tortuous so called medical
treatments went hand-in-hand

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.060
with the tyranny of oral teaching

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:57.700
in the centuries before the Enlightenment.

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:01.020
These amounted to trials by
ordeal, yielding considerable

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:04.800
suffering, illness, and
sometimes the death.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.070
The premise was that
drilling, cutting, fracturing,

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:11.480
scorching, or poisoning
would open up the ear

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.773
and the brain to sounds.

00:17:15.830 --> 00:17:18.520
It was into this oral world that arrived

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:21.120
the Abbé de L'Épée, a hearing priest

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:22.323
about to make history.

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:26.090
Observing Deaf twin girls
signing to each other

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:29.940
in 1755, he suspected that their signs

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:34.540
were mutually recognizable
representations of ideas.

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.340
Going a step further, he wondered whether

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.620
just as hearing children
are taught to write

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.670
what they hear, so these
children might be taught

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:46.130
to write what they see, I.E. to be taught

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:48.849
in their own signs the
meaning of written words

00:17:48.849 --> 00:17:51.913
and to learn how to read
and write those words.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.160
L'Épée established a school
for the Deaf in Paris

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:00.810
in 1755 and the royal government
ultimately granted him

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.740
a substantial annual stipend to run it.

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:06.300
But for all his perceptiveness,
it was the first time

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:08.188
anybody had done this.

00:18:08.188 --> 00:18:10.330
L'Épée didn't become fluent in signs

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:13.560
and never understood it
was a complete language.

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:16.270
He gave the students his
own supplementary signs

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:18.660
rather than learning
from them and mastering

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:20.193
their own integral tongue.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:26.140
He died in 1789 but in 1794,
the government gave his school

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:29.190
its imposing home on the rue Saint-Jacques

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:30.570
where it is today.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.100
The National Institute for Deaf Children,

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:34.700
called Saint-Jacques.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.520
It was to become and
today remains the beacon

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:39.363
of the age of the Enlightenment.

00:18:40.670 --> 00:18:43.490
August Bébian who was hearing, and perhaps

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:46.020
history's greatest teacher of the Deaf

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.160
was born the year L'Épée died.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:50.750
He was sent by his father from Guadeloupe

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:53.510
where he was born to study at

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:55.990
the (speaking in foreign
language) in Paris.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:58.540
His parents were friends of
the director of Saint-Jacques

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:01.470
who offered to find a
place for him to live.

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:04.680
Bébian lived near the
school and in the afternoons

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:10.000
and during the vacations
he was constantly here

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.150
joining the Deaf students
in their classes,

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.290
workshops, and games.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:16.500
Although hearing, he
made friends with several

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:19.960
of the Deaf students and
thanks to Jean Massieu,

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.740
a noted Deaf teacher
there, learned to sign

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.670
as well as those who were Deaf from birth.

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:27.563
An unprecedented accomplishment.

00:19:28.705 --> 00:19:30.960
Bébian's writings and
teachings were to become

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.370
the cornerstone of the
education of the Deaf

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:39.370
in Europe and of the Deaf
in the United States.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.300
What was needed, he
realized, in order to teach

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.690
his students to read and write was

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:48.320
the immediate translation of
their own thoughts and images

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.360
as expressed in their own language

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.800
into the corresponding
written words in French.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:57.200
Central to his thought in
teaching is the reality

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:01.240
that words are about the
expression of pre-existing ideas.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.426
An idea, whether the
conception of a material object

00:20:04.426 --> 00:20:08.240
or an abstract thought
necessarily precedes

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:10.183
the spoken word interpreted.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:14.050
It is thus the idea, he
wrote, that must give the word

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:17.660
meaning before the word can,
in turn, become an effective

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:21.160
interpreter of the
idea, or what John Locke

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:25.423
had interestingly called
the sign of the idea.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:30.630
The hearing ordinarily convey
their thoughts by speaking,

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.130
or by writing down words made of letters

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:35.050
that are depictions of the sound we make

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:36.440
with our voices.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:41.260
What Bébian eloquently called
the painting of our speech.

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:43.400
While students arriving at
Saint-Jacques would have

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:45.827
at least the rudiments of sign,

00:20:45.827 --> 00:20:48.410
Bébian in his practical teaching manual

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:51.960
hypothesized the case of a
new student at Saint-Jacques

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:55.130
in order to illustrate
for parents and teachers

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:58.150
that even the teaching of individual words

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:01.860
should begin with the
thought, not the written word.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.940
The teacher might show the
picture of a saber in a book

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.730
and then move his dominant
hand from opposite waist

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:10.630
to shoulder height as if drawing a saber

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:11.790
from its sheath.

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:12.950
Exaggerating the sign,

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:15.273
remember they're not recognizable iconic.

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:18.510
The student would invariably do the same.

00:21:19.858 --> 00:21:22.450
Bébian would unsheathe
the imaginary saber again

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.580
and have the student show
him its picture in the book.

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:27.580
Whereupon he would point
to the written word

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.270
beneath the picture.

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:31.610
He would then have the
student finger spell the word

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:34.310
if he knew finger spelling
and ask him in sign

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:35.963
what the word meant, what.

00:21:40.871 --> 00:21:42.010
The student would then repeat the gesture

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:44.610
and before long would understand
that this written word

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:47.490
and others were a kind
of conventional drawing

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:49.853
of the idea first expressed in sign.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:53.620
As to ideas expressed in whole sentences,

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:57.060
Bébian knew that the apparent
goal before his time,

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:59.620
a barren goal before
his time, and continuing

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:02.650
to his own day was to give
to the Deaf and I quote

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:05.270
the mechanical faculty of speech

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:08.540
without enabling them
to attach a precise idea

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.800
to its components,
leaving them in the dark

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:15.340
not only as to the absolute value of words

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:18.140
but their relative value
and the influence they have

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:22.040
over each other in the
composition of a phrase.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:25.090
Hence the drill even today of parents

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:27.150
and speech therapists hammering home

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:30.219
the pronunciation and lip
reading of multiple words

00:22:30.219 --> 00:22:35.219
by Deaf children have a
necessity got things backwards.

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:39.080
For without Sign Language, it is difficult

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:45.210
if not impossible to
convey ideas and syntax

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:47.653
and whole meanings of sentences.

00:22:48.654 --> 00:22:51.410
Bébian thus correctly
emphasized that French grammar,

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:54.120
which he described as the
relationship between words

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.700
and our spoken languages had to be taught

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:59.430
through the median of Sign Language.

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:02.340
He said the Deaf student must
be able to write the word

00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:05.100
in order to read it and
to be able to read it

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:08.729
he has to understand it and
he'll be able to understand it

00:23:08.729 --> 00:23:12.120
only if he understands the whole sentence.

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:15.343
To do that, his instruction
must begin in sign.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.300
Writing for the Deaf
pre-aphasia represented

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:22.820
nothing at all.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:24.880
It consists of lines or strokes

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:26.600
forming meaningless characters.

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:28.620
More complex than their painting of sounds

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:30.980
for the hearing, and far more removed

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:33.810
from the underlying
idea that its expression

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:37.130
in their native language
and in trying to read lips

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:39.840
the Deaf are not receiving any idea

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:42.694
but are forced to observe
obscure lip movements

00:23:42.694 --> 00:23:46.590
in order to guess sounds they cannot hear

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:49.760
so that they may infer the other sounds

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:52.180
that the lips failed to show at all.

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:57.180
The mental torture of Bonet
and his Oralist successors.

00:23:57.780 --> 00:23:59.890
If the Deaf student,
Bébian wrote, is brought to

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:04.510
a standstill to begin with
by the singular difficulty

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:07.008
of pronouncing sounds of which the signs

00:24:07.008 --> 00:24:11.100
fixed on paper lie
stationary before his eyes,

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:14.720
how is he going to
understand the rapid signs

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:16.623
that slip through the lips?

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:20.427
It is with their own
language that the education

00:24:20.427 --> 00:24:23.563
of the Deaf must begin,
not with the spoken word.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:30.656
We know, Bébian wrote, that
the Deaf have a language

00:24:30.656 --> 00:24:32.890
one doesn't teach them.

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:36.030
Although with art and
exercise, one can offer it

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:38.400
the happiest development.

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:42.820
It is, in a way, the
reflection of their sensations,

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:45.290
the relief of their oppressions.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:48.250
We carry the same timeless
and limitless principle

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:51.360
within all of us that
of the first language

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:54.180
of any human being which
gives immediate expression

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.840
to his thought and is not a translation

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:59.190
of any other language but expresses

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:01.700
his intimate connection with ideas.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:03.800
One ever looks to his first language

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:07.490
with difficulty for the
expression of an idea.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:11.370
The thought born in the brain bursts forth

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:14.463
like a flame sparkling in crystal.

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:17.990
How effective was Bébian?

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:20.720
In 1789 at the time of L'Épée's death

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.520
there were six schools
for the Deaf in France.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:26.530
By 1858, there were 54 such schools

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:29.330
and sign was the language of
instruction in all of them.

00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:32.040
A significant number of
teachers for the Deaf

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:33.840
were Deaf themselves.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:37.430
Deaf education and literacy
were springing up everywhere.

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:39.420
With Deaf children in Europe reading

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.480
(speaking in foreign language)

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:45.750
as well as Augustine's
Spiritual Dimensions.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:49.009
Proclaiming that the Deaf
would never be able to read

00:25:49.009 --> 00:25:52.563
the very book they were
reading or any other.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:56.760
On the more practical side,
the potential of the Deaf

00:25:56.760 --> 00:26:00.288
in the arts and sciences and
commerce were now realizable

00:26:00.288 --> 00:26:03.220
and signed languages
were becoming the motor

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:04.840
of their equality.

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:07.820
Together, Bébian and his
Deaf colleagues and students

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:09.420
were changing the world.

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:12.410
Students were entering the
professions of the hearing,

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:14.990
including work in lawyer offices, banks,

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:17.180
trading companies, et cetera.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:20.060
Some were accomplished painters and poets.

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:23.650
It wasn't a perfect world but
now they had two languages,

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:25.420
French Sign Language and the written

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:28.059
and European Sign Language.

00:26:28.059 --> 00:26:30.451
Languages, pardon me.

00:26:30.451 --> 00:26:32.730
The written language of the hearing.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:35.610
They were succeeding in the hearing world.

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:36.670
What happened in America?

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:39.950
Well, we were, of course,
seeking solutions too

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:43.680
and in 1816, as most of us here know,

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.530
Thomas Gallaudet of
Hartford came to France

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:48.890
looking for one, a solution.

00:26:48.890 --> 00:26:51.360
Leaving London after the
British wanted to charge him

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:55.810
for teaching him their
ineffective oral methods,

00:26:55.810 --> 00:26:58.430
which they maintained were secret.

00:26:58.430 --> 00:27:00.640
He went to Saint-Jacques
and took back to the States

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:04.470
with him a prominent
teacher named Laurant Clerc.

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:06.950
Clerc and Gallaudet brought
Bébian's teaching with him

00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:09.410
to the United States,
with which they formed

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:11.740
the Deaf core of American teachers.

00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:13.870
English immigrants had given us our spoken

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:16.700
and written language, but
the French and the person

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.810
of Laurent Clerc enriched
American Sign Language

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:23.870
with French Sign and used a
new combined language, ASL,

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:27.180
to teach the American
Deaf the written language

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:29.373
of France's rival across the channel.

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:34.930
By the 1850's, some
250 of the 550 teachers

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:38.170
and administrators in the
burgeoning national network

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:40.310
of schools were themselves Deaf.

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:43.590
By the 1860's, there was
26 schools and sign was

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:45.723
the language of instruction of all.

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:49.820
But for a variety of reasons,
the champions of speech

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:51.963
teaching would never let go.

00:27:53.026 --> 00:27:55.270
Bébian's Deaf pupil, Ferdinand Berthier,

00:27:55.270 --> 00:27:59.160
the greatest Deaf teacher of the Deaf

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:01.060
and Deaf leader of the 19th century

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:02.530
was well aware of it.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:04.293
He did a lot to try to stop it.

00:28:08.196 --> 00:28:10.900
One of the most beautiful
things he did, I think,

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:15.040
was to organize annual Deaf
banquets that were held

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:19.190
all around Paris between
1832 and 1869 or so.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:26.240
He invited the greats to come
(speaking in foreign language)

00:28:27.049 --> 00:28:28.514
and many others.

00:28:28.514 --> 00:28:31.570
Like today, he had
children of Deaf parents

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:34.040
who were hearing do the interpreting

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:37.040
so that everybody could
understand everybody else

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:39.563
just as we are doing today.

00:28:41.531 --> 00:28:42.781
Berthier was also a poet.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:48.090
I'll quote this poem because I love it.

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:50.100
It's much better than
my English translation

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:51.330
but here goes.

00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:53.440
As background, the Phoenician alphabet,

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:56.330
about 1000 B.C. is the source

00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:58.880
of virtually all modern alphabets.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:00.980
Berthier himself referred to this point

00:29:00.980 --> 00:29:04.370
in his own poem pronouncing
in Sign Language

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:07.023
and interpreted by another
at one of these banquets.

00:29:08.440 --> 00:29:10.290
Here it is: we do not speak, it is true,

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:13.890
but still, do you think us
unable to express ourselves

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:18.890
as well with our eyes, our
hands, our smiles, our lips?

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:22.310
Our most beautiful discourse
is at the tips of our fingers

00:29:22.310 --> 00:29:25.300
and our language is
rich in secret beauties

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:27.703
that you who speak will never know.

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:32.560
Have we not, have we not,
our own art of Phoenecian

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:35.770
to paint the words that
speak into our eyes?

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:40.370
Your arts and sciences, safe or sound,

00:29:40.370 --> 00:29:43.010
are they not open to our ardent eyes?

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:46.540
Show us the heavens,
ambitious airs of Acarus

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:48.663
that I cannot ascend with you.

00:29:49.817 --> 00:29:53.130
But as I say, Berthier was well aware

00:29:53.130 --> 00:29:54.970
of the forces against him.

00:29:54.970 --> 00:29:57.650
He warned his colleagues
and students alike

00:29:57.650 --> 00:30:00.080
that if they didn't
fight for their education

00:30:00.080 --> 00:30:03.813
in their own language,
they would lose everything.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:12.780
After Bébian, the 19th
century Oralist movement

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:18.380
deliberately set out to
destroy the languages

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:21.603
and educational institutions of the Deaf.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:25.230
In fact, the movement all but ruined

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:28.240
the extraordinary accomplishments
of the great European

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:32.440
and American teachers of the
earlier part of the century.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:35.440
The Oralists were lead
by Alexander Graham Bell

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:37.820
and by hearing teachers of the Deaf

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:40.523
and others in Europe
and the United States.

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:46.150
At the 1880 Congress of Milan,
as many of us here know,

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:49.870
a gathering of hearing educators
went with little knowledge

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:51.143
of the signed languages.

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:55.890
The assembly proudly
proclaimed the incontestable

00:30:55.890 --> 00:31:00.670
superiority of speech
over sign and resolved

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:03.940
that Deaf students
should be taught only by

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:06.580
the pure oral method.

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:09.640
Before long, throughout
Europe, speech became

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:13.070
virtually the sole method of teaching.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:16.250
By requiring Deaf students
to hear or lip read

00:31:16.250 --> 00:31:19.070
and to speak, impossible tasks.

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:21.010
Virtually impossible tasks.

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:23.137
Milan made so faculty...

00:31:24.517 --> 00:31:27.423
Milan made the sole
faculty the students lacked

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:30.293
the instrument of their instruction.

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:33.330
What was the motive of the Oralists?

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:36.193
Why insist on teaching a
child words he couldn't speak

00:31:36.193 --> 00:31:39.413
and staring at lips he
couldn't understand?

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:44.310
For some, notably the
oral teachers, the motive

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:47.240
was the desire to keep their jobs.

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:50.910
As to Bell, he conceded that Sign Language

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:53.520
was the quickest method
of reaching the mind

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:57.620
of a Deaf child, but it was
not the mind of a Deaf child

00:31:57.620 --> 00:31:58.803
that interested Bell.

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:03.470
Quote: the production of a defective race

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:07.160
of signing human beings,
he wrote, would be

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:09.423
a great calamity to the world.

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:13.010
If they were deprived of
their language of signs,

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:16.070
the Deaf would be unable
to understand not only

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:18.550
the hearing but each other.

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:21.240
They would marry less,
have fewer children,

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:22.993
and their number would diminish.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:26.900
Even Bell's prejudice ideas about eugenics

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:30.030
were without foundation
for as mentioned earlier

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:33.830
about 95% of children
born Deaf then and today

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:36.233
are born of hearing parents.

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:41.030
Bell and his allies were
extraordinarily successful.

00:32:41.030 --> 00:32:45.230
In 1867, American Sign
Language had been the median

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.640
of instruction in all
educational institutions

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.040
for the Deaf.

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:55.013
By 1927, it was used in virtually none.

00:32:56.110 --> 00:32:58.590
Deaf students were graduating
from Oralist schools

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:02.450
in Europe and America as
functional illiterates

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:06.010
in the written languages
of their native countries.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:09.060
Gallaudet College was
discouraging its students

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:10.970
from becoming teachers.

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:15.970
In France, the country of
Bébian, as recently as 2015

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:21.183
a prominent ear surgeon
wrote in Le Figaro,

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:26.530
that quote: speech is the only language

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:29.623
the brain can comprehend, unquote.

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:33.513
Finally, some brighter sides.

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:39.300
The eminent linguist, Noam
Chomsky, giving a lecture

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:43.600
in Chicago in 1965,
mistakenly, that's so rare

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:46.740
that Chomsky makes a
mistake, defined language

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:50.840
as a specific sound to
meaning correspondence.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:52.980
When asked where this
left the sign languages

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:55.360
of the Deaf, Chomsky
revised his definition

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:59.210
on the spot calling
language a specific signal

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:01.350
to meaning correspondence.

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:03.756
The distinction was
important for it was offered

00:34:03.756 --> 00:34:07.330
at a time when William
Stokoe and his colleagues

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:10.850
at Gallaudet University
were rediscovering,

00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:14.100
they were rediscovering,
what had been known in France

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:18.060
and America in the early 19th century.

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:20.850
Much of what Stokoe wrote
about in linguistics

00:34:20.850 --> 00:34:25.320
was really taken from
Bébian's own writings.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:27.890
Stokoe gave him credit, it
was very inconspicuous credit,

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:29.320
but he did give Bébian credit.

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:31.740
So these ideas that are coming up today,

00:34:31.740 --> 00:34:33.580
they're not new ideas.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.410
These were ideas that were developed

00:34:35.410 --> 00:34:38.246
in the early 19th century
and were suppressed

00:34:38.246 --> 00:34:41.570
with the money of Bell
and the United States,

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:44.030
with the Perriere's in
Europe, with the help of

00:34:44.030 --> 00:34:46.399
the Oralist teachers
and the Oralist movement

00:34:46.399 --> 00:34:50.110
and they basically smothered what we knew

00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:52.470
so much so that when
people rediscovered it

00:34:52.470 --> 00:34:55.180
in the 20th century, they
thought they were discovering

00:34:55.180 --> 00:34:57.513
something new and they weren't.

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:03.620
Chomsky's work and the work
of Stokoe and his successors

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:06.080
were of exceptional significance.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:08.160
This was not so...

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:10.430
This was so not simply
because the hearing world

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:14.023
had forgotten what it
had known by the 1820's,

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:18.330
but because of the calamitous success

00:35:18.330 --> 00:35:20.610
as I just said, and I'm repeating myself,

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.810
of the Oralist movement.

00:35:22.910 --> 00:35:25.810
Have the thoughts and work of
Chomsky, Stokoe, and others

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:29.413
now helped to overcome the
educational plight of the Deaf?

00:35:30.750 --> 00:35:33.130
They've unquestionably
led to a renaissance

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:37.420
of linguistic and educational
interest in sign languages,

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:41.580
giving new life, including
now our academic life

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:45.793
here at Yale, to the
seminal work of Bébian.

00:35:46.940 --> 00:35:50.580
It is now broadly understood
that Chomsky's language faculty

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:54.000
operates with equal facility,
whether stimulated by

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:56.390
language heard by the ears of the hearing

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:58.650
or beheld by the eyes of the Deaf.

00:35:58.650 --> 00:36:03.360
The primitive notions of
the Deaf and their language

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:06.130
espoused at Milan and promoted by Bell

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:10.000
are now rather thoroughly discredited

00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:14.283
but the educational problems
of the Deaf persist today.

00:36:15.370 --> 00:36:17.790
80% of newborn Deaf babies in the west

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:20.670
are being given cochlear implants.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:23.910
The implants are devices,
as most of us know,

00:36:23.910 --> 00:36:26.930
that produce hearing bypassing the ear

00:36:26.930 --> 00:36:29.790
and delivering electronic
signals directly to

00:36:29.790 --> 00:36:31.333
the auditory nerve.

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.500
The benefits of cochlear
implants are measured today

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:39.580
in a laboratory setting
and alas, appear to fall

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:42.740
far short outside that setting.

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:45.620
Thus, the real world
of daily communication

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:49.263
is still a struggle despite the device.

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:54.383
Notably, for children, who are born Deaf.

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:58.333
Language can be said to give all of us,

00:36:59.415 --> 00:37:04.415
whether Deaf or hearing,
our modern soul in the age

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:09.950
of science and to underpin
our rights as individuals.

00:37:09.950 --> 00:37:12.891
Whether that soul can best
be expressed and understood

00:37:12.891 --> 00:37:16.290
and can best understand
the world around it

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:20.410
in its natural language,
or with cochlear implants,

00:37:20.410 --> 00:37:24.963
or perhaps both, is the
heart and soul of the matter.

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:30.120
Is a smaller, fully
communicative world preferable

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:32.403
to a larger, poorly communicative one?

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:35.393
Is there a both worlds alternative?

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:39.330
Are the implants a paradigmatic change

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:42.176
or are they an extension
of the dramatic course

00:37:42.176 --> 00:37:47.176
of 19th and 20th century Deaf
history and of earlier times?

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:51.100
There are a variety of views

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:53.700
on these hotly debated questions.

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:57.340
My own views are shaped
both by my own research,

00:37:57.340 --> 00:38:00.180
by careful study of
published test results,

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:02.990
and naturally enough, by
my intimate acquaintance

00:38:02.990 --> 00:38:04.380
with the history.

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:08.116
I propose no answers here,
but whatever the answers

00:38:08.116 --> 00:38:10.839
it is critically important that we ensure

00:38:10.839 --> 00:38:15.470
that these children going Deaf be given

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:20.020
the opportunity to learn
as soon as possible in life

00:38:20.020 --> 00:38:23.796
what is their own, their
first, their natural language

00:38:23.796 --> 00:38:28.220
and advocation of the soul itself.

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:29.791
Thank you very much.

00:38:29.791 --> 00:38:32.791
(audience applauds)

00:38:42.660 --> 00:38:44.163
- Thank you very much.

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:48.050
We now have some time for questions.

00:38:48.050 --> 00:38:51.230
I suggest that we turn
your question period

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:56.160
into the question period for everyone.

00:38:56.160 --> 00:39:00.580
So please feel free to ask questions

00:39:00.580 --> 00:39:03.527
for our last speaker but also
for the other two panelists.

00:39:03.527 --> 00:39:05.323
Okay.

00:39:06.485 --> 00:39:08.118
We have two microphones
that will run up and down

00:39:08.118 --> 00:39:11.543
the aisles to receive your questions.

00:39:16.150 --> 00:39:19.153
Okay, see, we have a question here.

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:25.140
- [Audience Member] Hi, thank
you all for speaking today.

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:26.750
It was great.

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:28.910
Anyone can answer this but
it's a question regarding

00:39:28.910 --> 00:39:30.450
the language acquisition.
- Be a little louder.

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:31.283
- [Audience Member] Oh,
it's a question regarding

00:39:31.283 --> 00:39:33.330
the language acquisition.

00:39:33.330 --> 00:39:36.040
I was wondering like we're
talking about in class

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:38.720
most if not all users
of sign are bilingual.

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:41.010
They have their language of sign and also

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:43.170
their written language
because Sign Language

00:39:43.170 --> 00:39:45.010
doesn't have a written form.

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:48.320
So how are the acquisition
processes for these different?

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:51.325
Can you guys speak to your
personal experiences with this?

00:39:51.325 --> 00:39:54.130
Because there's an understanding
of an inborn filter

00:39:54.130 --> 00:39:56.690
for language, but does it work differently

00:39:56.690 --> 00:39:58.223
for sign versus writing?

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:02.160
- [Woman] So I think the question is:

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:05.770
is the acquisition
process in Sign Language

00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:08.910
different from the language
acquisition process

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:10.890
in spoken languages?

00:40:10.890 --> 00:40:11.950
- [Audience Member] No,
for when you're learning

00:40:11.950 --> 00:40:14.220
how to write the written language.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:17.040
Because there's a written English language

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:18.790
versus a Sign Language.

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.750
Most users of sign are
bilingual in that sense.

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:25.429
So is a language acquisition
process for that different

00:40:25.429 --> 00:40:26.793
and how does that work?

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:36.680
- [Interpreter] I can
speak a little bit to that.

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:39.880
I don't specialize in
literacy of Deaf children

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:44.270
but I do know a little bit
about the research that exists.

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:46.310
There are studies, a
great deal of studies,

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:47.143
looking at that.

00:40:50.130 --> 00:40:53.667
There are, we know, Deaf
students who are very successful

00:40:53.667 --> 00:40:55.950
in terms of literacy skills.

00:40:55.950 --> 00:40:58.733
The question is how do they
become such good readers?

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:03.200
And how do they become
literate in a written language

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:07.230
that does not apply to their
Sign Language abilities?

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:08.733
It's entirely separate.

00:41:15.239 --> 00:41:17.240
Because the written
language does not track

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:19.140
with the Sign Language that they use.

00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:20.580
Let me just back up for just a minute.

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:24.490
For hearing children,
learning a spoken language

00:41:24.490 --> 00:41:28.200
tracks with the written
language that they also learn.

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:32.830
There's much research that
shows phonological knowledge

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:35.330
of language really boosts
the ability to learn

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:36.630
to read and write.

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:41.630
So a lot of literacy instruction
has to do with phonology

00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:46.713
and how that plays into
learning to read and write.

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:52.423
That phonology does not
map of the written language

00:41:52.423 --> 00:41:56.460
does not map onto a Sign
Language used by Deaf students.

00:41:56.460 --> 00:41:59.540
Research today is looking
at how Deaf students

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:03.540
may capitalize on the phonology
of their Sign Language

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:08.220
to assist in the process,
or support the process,

00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:09.730
of learning to read and write.

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:14.730
Not all of the literacy
applies to Deaf children

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:18.560
in this area but there are
some findings that we can apply

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:22.280
to the situation in Deaf education.

00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:24.270
So there's some evidence
that the morphology

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:28.640
of sign languages in having
phonology a foundation

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:32.020
in one language can be used
to enhance the language

00:42:32.020 --> 00:42:35.740
learning process in literacy
of a second language.

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:36.810
- [Interpreter] I'd like to add one thing

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:38.400
to what Amber said.

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:42.610
So every child, regardless
of if they're learning

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:45.070
a spoken language or a Sign
Language has to be taught

00:42:45.070 --> 00:42:46.620
how to read and write.

00:42:46.620 --> 00:42:48.980
The writing systems aren't universal,

00:42:48.980 --> 00:42:50.590
they're a cultural invention.

00:42:50.590 --> 00:42:53.540
So it's always the case
that the process of learning

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:55.240
how to read and write is different

00:42:55.240 --> 00:42:56.850
from language acquisition.

00:42:56.850 --> 00:43:00.480
So the question is how is it
different for Sign Language

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:03.410
and visual language users,
versus spoken language users?

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.860
Because there's not a one-to-one mapping

00:43:05.860 --> 00:43:09.780
in phonological firm and
that's the research question.

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:11.130
That we have not yet...

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:12.960
Well, we're still looking at now.

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:15.790
It's a current area of research.

00:43:15.790 --> 00:43:18.640
- As to syntax, Bébian
made a wonderful point

00:43:18.640 --> 00:43:23.370
in teaching kids that he
would first go through

00:43:23.370 --> 00:43:26.160
the notion of a sentence.

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:27.540
This table, I strike.

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:30.290
I think in those days, I don't
know whether it's the case

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:33.630
now but the sentence would often begin

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:34.560
with the object.

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:39.430
So a signing student
would be signed by Bébian

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:41.260
this table, I strike.

00:43:41.260 --> 00:43:44.170
He'd make sure the student got the idea

00:43:44.170 --> 00:43:46.940
and then having been through the exercise

00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:50.420
of teaching the child words and sentences

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:53.080
he would say well, when you write it down

00:43:53.080 --> 00:43:55.470
you don't write this table, I strike.

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:58.230
You write I strike this table.

00:43:58.230 --> 00:44:01.586
This is the way these
crazy hearing people do it.

00:44:01.586 --> 00:44:03.947
(audience laughing)

00:44:03.947 --> 00:44:07.630
He was quite good I
think at getting syntax

00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:09.330
and grammar through to those kids.

00:44:17.698 --> 00:44:20.448
- There's a question next to you.

00:44:22.491 --> 00:44:23.500
- [Audience Member] A
friend of mine online

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:27.420
was upset with the
cochlear marketing where

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:30.053
children were not supposed
to be taught sign.

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:35.637
She herself was Deaf and she
was very offended with this

00:44:35.637 --> 00:44:37.340
and I can certainly understand

00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:40.013
her attitude towards genocide.

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:44.573
- [Woman] Towards, what did you say?

00:44:46.090 --> 00:44:47.690
- [Audience Member] Genocide,
that's where you discriminate

00:44:47.690 --> 00:44:49.330
an entire cultures.

00:44:49.330 --> 00:44:50.210
That's what the cochlear people early on

00:44:50.210 --> 00:44:51.880
were trying to do.

00:44:51.880 --> 00:44:55.100
It's a natural, very typical,

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:59.030
natural asinine sort of thing people do.

00:44:59.030 --> 00:45:00.743
They wanted to help people so of course

00:45:00.743 --> 00:45:03.863
they had to exterminate
other efforts to help.

00:45:06.879 --> 00:45:09.740
But looking into this,
it looks to me as though

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:13.350
baby sign is very beneficial
to the intellectual

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:16.540
and spiritual development
of small children

00:45:16.540 --> 00:45:18.953
at a very important age of development.

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:23.430
A child who can express
himself at the age of one

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:27.150
is going to be developing
rhetorical abilities

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:33.190
an entire year before most
children can make noises

00:45:33.190 --> 00:45:34.390
come out of their faces.

00:45:36.970 --> 00:45:40.903
But now, this evening,
I understand baby sign

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:44.670
could be significant towards the training

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:48.493
of future engineers because
of the spatial aspect.

00:45:51.568 --> 00:45:53.531
I say that very seriously.

00:45:53.531 --> 00:45:56.480
A moment ago we were talking
about the inadequacies

00:45:56.480 --> 00:45:59.780
of written language to
which I would respond

00:45:59.780 --> 00:46:01.460
that there is also a subject of drafting

00:46:01.460 --> 00:46:04.390
which could expend written language,

00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:06.550
could be seen as
expanding written language

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:08.723
in the direction of spatial orientation.

00:46:12.255 --> 00:46:13.610
So it seems to me that baby sign

00:46:13.610 --> 00:46:15.083
is a very important subject.

00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:19.310
Sign Language should not be confined

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:24.080
to special education or
of linguistic rarities

00:46:24.080 --> 00:46:26.003
but should be learned by every parent.

00:46:27.610 --> 00:46:28.740
Beyond that, I would like to know

00:46:28.740 --> 00:46:30.513
how do I say Nicaragua?

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:33.650
That seems to be very
important this evening.

00:46:33.650 --> 00:46:34.483
Nicaragua.

00:46:35.360 --> 00:46:37.740
- How do you say what?
- Nicaragua.

00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:38.940
- [Woman] Oh, Nicaragua.

00:46:40.477 --> 00:46:41.693
Thank you for, yeah.

00:46:50.385 --> 00:46:51.460
I guess it was a simple question

00:46:51.460 --> 00:46:55.080
how do you say Nicaragua
and a longer question

00:46:55.080 --> 00:46:57.783
about the importance of signs.

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:09.700
- [Interpreter] If you're
asking a specific question

00:47:09.700 --> 00:47:14.180
about baby sign, I can't answer that

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:16.500
without first saying that

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:20.963
we can't talk about baby
signs for hearing children,

00:47:22.450 --> 00:47:26.523
unless we also are
talking about education,

00:47:27.680 --> 00:47:29.680
teaching Sign Language to Deaf children.

00:47:31.240 --> 00:47:36.240
So I would say issues about
teaching hearing infants

00:47:36.550 --> 00:47:38.373
to sign, okay.

00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:41.960
That's an individual
decision for parents to make

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:47.720
but we can't talk about that first.

00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:50.130
I think first we need to address the fact

00:47:50.130 --> 00:47:53.550
that Deaf children need to
have equal opportunities.

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:55.600
That's where the focus needs to be first.

00:48:04.913 --> 00:48:07.231
(audience laughing)

00:48:07.231 --> 00:48:09.481
(mumbling)

00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:13.823
This is the sign for Nicaragua.

00:48:14.850 --> 00:48:15.683
Nicaragua.

00:48:18.521 --> 00:48:19.403
In NSL.

00:48:22.018 --> 00:48:24.768
(woman laughing)

00:48:34.480 --> 00:48:36.680
- [Audience Member] I have a question for

00:48:36.680 --> 00:48:39.100
particularly for Dr. Martin and colleagues

00:48:39.100 --> 00:48:44.100
about the relationship between
the origins of language

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.480
and special works.

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:49.440
So one of the interesting things
in the archeological record

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:54.440
is that with a stone tool
traditions we see stone tools

00:48:55.690 --> 00:48:59.120
from a couple of million years
ago and they're unchanged,

00:48:59.120 --> 00:49:02.940
unchanged, unchanged, and
then get bifacial blades

00:49:02.940 --> 00:49:06.920
the closer we get,
symmetrical stone flakes,

00:49:06.920 --> 00:49:10.360
and then they stay unchanged,
unchanged, unchanged

00:49:10.360 --> 00:49:12.180
and so on for another half a million years

00:49:12.180 --> 00:49:15.170
and then we see a stone
tool specialization,

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:19.650
we see different shapes
of tools and so on.

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:23.600
So of course the production
of the bifacial blades,

00:49:23.600 --> 00:49:26.350
the ones with the chips on either side,

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:30.470
requires being able to manipulate tools

00:49:30.470 --> 00:49:34.250
and think about symmetry at the same time.

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:38.680
So I'm wondering if you see
that as a potential clue

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:43.300
to language development or
whether it's something like

00:49:43.300 --> 00:49:46.580
the later proliferation of stone tools

00:49:46.580 --> 00:49:49.070
and the variation and so on
which might be more indicative.

00:49:49.070 --> 00:49:50.330
Is there any thoughts
you have on this matter

00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:51.163
would be great?

00:49:57.970 --> 00:50:01.430
- [Interpreter] Honestly,
I'm not sure exactly

00:50:01.430 --> 00:50:02.950
what you're asking.

00:50:02.950 --> 00:50:04.980
You're talking about
comparing the development

00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:09.220
of those two things over
time in the emergence

00:50:09.220 --> 00:50:14.187
of a language and how
spatial ideas like symmetry

00:50:15.459 --> 00:50:16.730
and things like that.

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:20.390
How they influence and shape language,

00:50:20.390 --> 00:50:22.650
is that what you're addressing?

00:50:22.650 --> 00:50:24.270
- [Audience Member] It's
more the question related

00:50:24.270 --> 00:50:29.270
to Dr. Kocab's point about
tracing language origins

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:32.670
and I guess cultural evolution

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:36.350
and biological evolution of language.

00:50:36.350 --> 00:50:39.060
So we see these I guess evidence.

00:50:39.060 --> 00:50:42.780
We see pieces of evidence
for cultural development

00:50:42.780 --> 00:50:46.941
over millions of years but
yet the work on Nicaragua

00:50:46.941 --> 00:50:50.788
and Sign Language suggests
that language development

00:50:50.788 --> 00:50:53.340
for these kids is very quick within, what?

00:50:53.340 --> 00:50:55.810
Two generations or three generations.

00:50:55.810 --> 00:50:59.900
So I'm wondering what lessons
we can learn about reading

00:50:59.900 --> 00:51:04.383
that contemporary situations
back into the remote past?

00:51:10.540 --> 00:51:12.596
Yeah, that's kind of
how I feel about it too.

00:51:12.596 --> 00:51:13.650
(audience laughing)

00:51:13.650 --> 00:51:15.500
- [Woman] A difficult question, yeah.

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:20.680
- [Interpreter] I have some thoughts.

00:51:20.680 --> 00:51:23.897
I'm not sure though if my thoughts though

00:51:23.897 --> 00:51:25.290
will actually answer your question.

00:51:25.290 --> 00:51:28.173
But would you like to take
a shot at that, Annemarie?

00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:32.160
Something that comes to mind for me is

00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:35.583
and I'm not sure if this is
exactly what you're asking.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:43.340
Part of what Dr. Kocab talked about

00:51:44.460 --> 00:51:47.610
in terms of cognitive tools
and resources that we have,

00:51:47.610 --> 00:51:50.860
those may have appeared long, long ago

00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:53.610
and those might be things
that we can take advantage of

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:56.733
to create language.

00:51:57.780 --> 00:52:02.240
So the languages that we create must...

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:05.740
Those cognitive resources must be in place

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:07.340
for that to occur.

00:52:07.340 --> 00:52:09.410
So the emergence of spatial language

00:52:09.410 --> 00:52:13.020
and how it emerges, for example,

00:52:13.020 --> 00:52:15.353
we see that for

00:52:19.500 --> 00:52:21.830
people learning Sign Language,

00:52:21.830 --> 00:52:24.053
well for all people, hearing or Deaf,

00:52:26.070 --> 00:52:29.720
there are strengths and weaknesses

00:52:29.720 --> 00:52:32.460
in terms of mental rotation capabilities.

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:36.460
What some of us are good
at, strength that we bring

00:52:36.460 --> 00:52:39.710
to the table, come into play when we think

00:52:39.710 --> 00:52:41.223
about human beings.

00:52:42.205 --> 00:52:46.210
For those who talk about other things,

00:52:46.210 --> 00:52:49.960
have other strengths and weaknesses,

00:52:49.960 --> 00:52:53.040
there may be something
about the language itself

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:57.510
that uses certain skills to incorporate

00:52:57.510 --> 00:52:59.770
into the development of the
language in the first place

00:52:59.770 --> 00:53:02.450
so that the kind of thing
you might be talking about

00:53:02.450 --> 00:53:06.660
is where long, long ago
historically perhaps

00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:10.390
a cognitive ability emerged at some point

00:53:10.390 --> 00:53:14.330
in the history of human
development and then there comes

00:53:14.330 --> 00:53:17.900
a time where language is needed
to capitalize on something

00:53:18.805 --> 00:53:21.930
to be able to do something
and then that feeds back

00:53:21.930 --> 00:53:24.210
into the development of
the language as a cycle.

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:25.950
That's the type of thing
I'm thinking about.

00:53:25.950 --> 00:53:28.760
I'm not sure if it addresses
what you're interested

00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:31.233
in knowing about but
those are my thoughts.

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:37.670
- [Interpreter] I'm
also not completely sure

00:53:37.670 --> 00:53:39.620
what the question was.

00:53:39.620 --> 00:53:44.290
Well, so you said that tools
